[0.296, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[H\r\u001b[6C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m\u001b[48;2;61;1;0m \u001b[11G\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mThe change is a p\u001b[29Gi\u001b[31Gacy\u001b[35Gfe\u001b[39Gure of\u001b[46Gthe game's own\u001b[61Gmultiplayer connection: it encrypts the\u001b[101Gtraffic\r\u001b[6C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m \u001b[11G\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mbetween\u001b[19Gthe\u001b[23Ggame's\u001b[30Gclient\u001b[37Gand\u001b[41Gthe\u001b[45Ggame's\u001b[52Gserver\u001b[59Gwith\u001b[64Glibsodium's\u001b[76Gdocumented\u001b[87Ghigh-level\u001b[98Gfunctions,\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m  7 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mso that a player's login token is not sent across the internet in plain text. It is ordinary           \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m  8 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mdefensive application code, the kind any online game ships, not security research and not a tool.      \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m  9 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m                                                                                                       \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m 10 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mWhy it needs review: encryption code fails silently. A key mixed up between the two directions, a      \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m 11 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mcounter that can repeat, or a check done in the wrong order still passes every test that sends a       \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m 12 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mmessage and reads it back, so a careful second reading is the only way such a bug is caught before     \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m 13 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mplayers rely on it. Your job is to find what is wrong with the change before it is committed: bugs,    \r\u001b[6C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m14\u001b[11G\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mbroken rules,\u001b[25Gclaims that do\u001b[40Gnot\u001b[44Ghold,\u001b[50Gand designs that will cost the project\u001b[89Glater. You