[0.257, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[H\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  \u001b[39mAdded \u001b[1m10\u001b[22m lines, removed \u001b[1m4\u001b[22m lines\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 210 \u001b[22m `Net::GenerateServerKey` makes a server's key pair, each key 64 lower-case hex characters, and\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 211 \u001b[22m `Net::PublicKeyOf` gives the public half of a secret one. The server is given the secret\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 212 \u001b[22m (`ServerConfig::secretKey`) and every client the public half (`ClientConfig::serverKey`); either\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m\u001b[48;2;61;1;0m 213 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mend refuses a key that is not 64 hex characters as `std::invalid_argument` when it is made.\u001b[48;2;92;2;0m The\u001b[48;2;61;1;0m       \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m 214 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mengine wipes the secrets it holds once they are spent: the server's text of its secret key once       \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m 215 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mread, each fresh key once the connection's keys are made, the credential once the hello is sent and   \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;220;90;90m 216 -\u001b[38;2;248;248;242monce it is judged, and a connection's keys when it closes. The caller's own copies are its own. Where \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m\u001b[48;2;2;40;0m 213 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mend refuses a key that is not 64 hex characters as `std::invalid_argument` when it is made.           \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 214 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m                                                                                                      \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 215 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mThe engine wipes the secrets it keeps once they are spent: the server's text of its secret key once   \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 216 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mread, each fresh key once the connection's keys are made, the credential once the hello is sealed,    \r\u001b[1B\u001b[49m\u001b[39m \u001b[3G   \u001b[38;2;80;200;80m\u001b[48;2;2;40;0m 217 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242monce it is judged and when a client is destroyed, and a connection's keys when it closes. A message   \r\u001b[1B\u001b[49m\u001b[39m     \u001b[38;2;80;200;80m\u001b[48;2;2;40;0m 218 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mis written into its packet and sealed there, so its plain text is not copied. That narrows what a     \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 219 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mlater look at the process's memory could find; it does not close it. The hello is encoded by the      \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 220 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mbinary archive, whose working buffers, and any buffer a growing vector leaves behind, go back to      \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 221 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mthe allocator unwiped, and the engine does not lock its memory against swap. The caller's own         \r\u001b[6C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m2\u001b[9G2\u001b[12G\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mcopies of\u001b[22Gits\u001b[26Gkeys and credentials are it\u001b[55Gown. Where  \u001b[68G   \u001b[72G            \u001b[85G  \u001b[88G   \u001b[92G           \u001b[104G     \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[49m\u001b[39m\u001b[2m 223 \u001b[22m the keys are kept and how a client is given its server's is the game's: a file the operator keeps\u001b[K\r\u001b[6C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m2\u001b[9G4\u001b[12G\u001b[22mprivate, and the publ\u001b[34Gc half in the\u001b[76Gs\u001b[78Gbuild. A s\u001b[89Gcret lost or leaked is\u001b[K\u001b[27;1H\u001b[24;3H\u001b[?25h"]
