[0.196, "o", "\u001b[?25l\u001b[H\r\u001b[1B     \u001b[38;2;80;200;80m\u001b[48;2;2;40;0m 114 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mkey could offer the pinned public key as its fresh one, and the hello it was sent would be sealed by  \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 115 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mthe server's secret alone, to be opened by whoever took that secret later.                            \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 116 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242m                                                                                                      \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m 117 +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mOnce the proof opens, the client sends its hello, sealed. It is a binary archive, format `jettison.hel\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;80;200;80m     +\u001b[38;2;248;248;242mlo` version 1,                                                                                        \r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[49m\u001b[39m\u001b[2m 118 \u001b[22m of the protocol's name, its version and the credential, so the credential never crosses the network\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 119 \u001b[22m in the clear. The hello's keys depend on the server's fresh key, so a hello recorded and replayed\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 120 \u001b[22m on another connection does not open. The server checks, in this order:\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;78;186;101m●\u001b[3G\u001b[39m\u001b[1mUpdate\u001b[22m(docs/networking.md)\u001b[K\r\u001b[1B\u001b[38;2;153;153;153m  ⎿  \u001b[39mAdded \u001b[1m10\u001b[22m lines, removed \u001b[1m4\u001b[22m lines\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 210 \u001b[22m `Net::GenerateServerKey` makes a server's key pair, each key 64 lower-case hex characters, and\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 211 \u001b[22m `Net::PublicKeyOf` gives the public half of a secret one. The server is given the secret\u001b[K\r\u001b[5C\u001b[1B\u001b[2m 212 \u001b[22m (`ServerConfig::secretKey`) and every clie